# Clean Code

## Objective

Write code that is easy to understand, modify, and maintain. Code is read far more than written  -  optimize for readability first.

## Readability First

Prefer clarity over cleverness. If two solutions perform equally, choose the easier to understand.

## Functions and Modules

- Each function should do one clearly defined task.
- Keep functions short; extract when logic branches multiply.
- Name functions and variables by intent, not implementation trivia.

## Naming

- Use consistent, descriptive names matching project conventions.
- Avoid abbreviations unless established in the codebase.
- Booleans and flags should read as questions or states (`isValid`, `hasPermission`).

## Comments

- Code should mostly explain itself.
- Comment non-obvious business rules, invariants, and thread/safety contracts.
- Do not narrate obvious syntax.

## Error Handling

- Handle errors at appropriate boundaries.
- Fail clearly; avoid silent swallowing unless documented.
- Cross-link: `security-first.mdc`, `production-ready.mdc`.

## Duplication

- Do not copy-paste logic  -  extract shared behavior when duplication is real.
- Cross-link: `code-reuse.mdc`.

